### Updates

Only `azure_params.content_type`, `azure_params.tags` and `azure_params.attributes` (`enabled`, `activation_date` and `expiration_date`) can be updated in place. Attributes marked as `(Immutable)`, including `azure_params.value`, cannot be modified after resource creation. Changing them is rejected at plan time and the resource is not recreated.

An update of `azure_params.tags` replaces the whole set of tags. To remove a tag, leave it out of the map. Removing `tags` from the configuration does not clear the tags in Azure, set `tags = {}` to remove them all.

An activation or expiration date cannot be cleared once it is set, and `expiration_date` must be later than `activation_date`.

To create a new version of a secret, create another secret resource with the same name.

### Destroy

Destroying the resource soft-deletes the secret in Azure. If `purge_secrets_on_delete` is true in the provider settings (the default) and the secret is purgeable, it is then purged. A purge straight after a soft-delete can return a conflict until Azure has finished, so it is retried until `azure_operation_timeout` is used up. If `purge_secrets_on_delete` is false, the secret is only soft-deleted.

If the secret is already soft-deleted or purged, only the remaining steps are done, so a destroy that failed part way can be run again. If the secret does not exist in CipherTrust Manager, destroy adds a warning and removes the resource from state. A read or update of a secret that does not exist in CipherTrust Manager is an error and the resource is kept in state. If it has been permanently deleted, remove it from the configuration or run `terraform state rm`.

### Creating a secret that is soft-deleted

If a soft-deleted secret of the same name exists in the vault, creating the resource recovers that secret instead of creating a new one. This requires `recover_soft_deleted_secrets` to be true in the provider settings. If it is false (the default), the create fails because the name is still held by the soft-deleted secret.

## Import

An Azure secret can be imported using its CipherTrust Manager secret ID. The ID is returned in the API response body as the `id` field and is also visible in the CCKM interface.

### Important: Behaviour after import

After import, Terraform will attempt to make the secret match your resource configuration. Only `azure_params.content_type`, `azure_params.tags` and `azure_params.attributes` can be updated. Any other difference is rejected at plan time and the resource is never replaced.

**`name`** and **`vault_id`** - returned by the API after import and must match the values in your configuration. `vault_id` is the CipherTrust Manager resource ID of the vault, not the vault name.

**`azure_params.value`** - input-only. The secret value is not read back, so it is `null` in state after import. You can leave it out of the configuration, or set it. When you set it, the value is kept in state as configured and no `ignore_changes` is needed. Once it is in state it is immutable.

**`azure_params.attributes`** - `enabled` is read back after import. The API does not return `activation_date` or `expiration_date`, so they are `null` in state after import. If you set either date in your configuration, Terraform plans an update to apply it. If you do not want Terraform to manage the dates yet, use `ignore_changes` as shown below.

**`azure_params.content_type`** and **`azure_params.tags`** - rebuilt from the secret after import. Set them in your configuration to match, or Terraform plans an update.

**`restore_secret`** - input-only and `null` in state after import. Omit it from your configuration.

**Soft-deleted secrets** - a secret that is soft-deleted cannot be imported as is. If `recover_soft_deleted_secrets` is true in the provider settings, the secret is recovered and then imported. If it is false (the default), the import fails.

To avoid unintended changes, do one of the following:

**Option 1 - Configure the resource block to match the secret's current state** before running `terraform apply`. Set `vault_id`, `name` and the `azure_params` values to their current values so that Terraform detects no diff.

**Option 2 - Use `lifecycle { ignore_changes }` to suppress diffs** on attributes you are not yet ready for Terraform to manage:

```terraform
lifecycle {
  ignore_changes = [
    azure_params,
  ]
}
```

To ignore only the activation and expiration dates, which are not returned by the API, and still manage the other attributes:

```terraform
lifecycle {
  ignore_changes = [
    azure_params.attributes.activation_date,
    azure_params.attributes.expiration_date,
  ]
}
```

The recommended approach (temporary use of `ignore_changes`):

1. Add the `lifecycle { ignore_changes }` block to the resource
2. Run `terraform apply` (or use `terraform import` for Terraform versions earlier than v1.5.0)
3. Run `terraform state show ciphertrust_azure_secret.example` to inspect the imported values
4. Update your resource configuration to match the imported state
5. Remove `ignore_changes` so Terraform manages the attributes going forward

You can import a resource into Terraform in two ways.
### 1. Import using an import block and terraform apply

Only available for Terraform v1.5.0 and later.

When Terraform processes the `import` block it:

1. Reads the secret from the API using the provided ID and stores the current values as state
2. Compares that state to your resource block configuration
3. Applies any differences as updates to the secret (immediately during the same apply)

All three steps happen within a single `terraform apply` run.

**Step 1 - Import with `ignore_changes`** (safe starting point):

```terraform
import {
  to = ciphertrust_azure_secret.example
  id = "abc12345-1234-1234-1234-123456789012"
}

resource "ciphertrust_azure_secret" "example" {
  vault_id = "<vault-resource-id>"
  name     = "<secret-name>"
  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [
      azure_params,
    ]
  }
}
```

**Step 2 - Final config after inspecting state** (remove `ignore_changes` once values are correct):

```terraform
resource "ciphertrust_azure_secret" "example" {
  vault_id = "<vault-resource-id>"
  name     = "<secret-name>"
  azure_params = {
    content_type = "password"
    attributes = {
      enabled         = true
      activation_date = "2026-07-03T14:24:00Z" # not returned by the API - set to the date in Azure
      expiration_date = "2030-07-03T14:24:00Z" # not returned by the API - set to the date in Azure
    }
    tags = {
      environment = "prod"
    }
  }
}
```

Include only the attributes that apply to your secret. For example, omit `azure_params.tags` if the secret has no tags, and omit the dates if none are set.

### 2. Import using the terraform import command

When `terraform import` is run it:

1. Reads the secret from the API using the provided ID and stores the current values as state
2. Writes the imported state to the state file - no plan or apply is performed

Before running `terraform apply`, apply Option 1 or Option 2 above to avoid unintended changes. Run `terraform plan` first to review exactly what would be applied.

```shell
terraform import ciphertrust_azure_secret.example abc12345-1234-1234-1234-123456789012
```

## Finding and restoring a purged secret

All versions of a secret share one backup, and a new backup is made each time a version is created. To find the versions of a secret by backup, filter the `ciphertrust_azure_secret_list` data source on the backup of the latest version:

```terraform
data "ciphertrust_azure_secret_list" "by_backup" {
  filters = {
    backup = ciphertrust_azure_secret.latest.backup
  }
}
```

After a secret is purged it is no longer in Terraform state. Restore it into a vault with `restore_secret`, using the CipherTrust Manager ID of the purged secret. `vault_id` can be any vault. `name` and `azure_params` cannot be set when restoring:

```terraform
resource "ciphertrust_azure_secret" "restored" {
  vault_id = "<vault-resource-id>"
  restore_secret = {
    secret_id = "<purged-secret-id>"
  }
}
```